1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,950
Hello, I’m Hayden.

2
00:00:01,950 --> 00:00:06,750
I’m one of the archaeologists investigating the site here at Bidcaster.

3
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:09,848
This is the third summer for this community project,

4
00:00:09,848 --> 00:00:13,670
and most of the people digging here are volunteers.

5
00:00:13,670 --> 00:00:17,623
I’m a full-time archaeologist for the town council,

6
00:00:17,623 --> 00:00:24,045
but I was asked to join the project by NHA, a charity which sets up projects like this up

7
00:00:24,045 --> 00:00:25,470
and down the country.

8
00:00:25,470 --> 00:00:29,329
As you can see, we’re next to Bidcaster Castle,

9
00:00:29,329 --> 00:00:33,650
which is great because the owners let us use their facilities.

10
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:37,490
So, how did we get to where we are today?

11
00:00:37,490 --> 00:00:42,025
Many archaeology projects happen when an ancient object is found,

12
00:00:42,025 --> 00:00:43,831
and in our case,

13
00:00:43,831 --> 00:00:46,740
that object was a gold coin.

14
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:52,458
Coins are often found by people using metal detectors to look for things buried in the ground –

15
00:00:52,458 --> 00:00:59,720
or coins are uncovered when wild animals like rabbits have been digging tunnels.

16
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:05,563
Here, a walker found it on the ground after a rainstorm washed away some of the earth

17
00:01:05,563 --> 00:01:07,350
and sand.

18
00:01:07,350 --> 00:01:12,484
When the story of the gold coin hit the news, Peter Swift, an amateur

19
00:01:12,484 --> 00:01:17,618
historian, contacted me to say he believed there had been a village on this

20
00:01:17,618 --> 00:01:21,770
site, centuries before the castle was built.

21
00:01:21,770 --> 00:01:26,843
Just by chance, the team found some old maps and documents in our library

22
00:01:26,843 --> 00:01:36,147
which showed five-hundred-year-old drawings of ruined buildings on the grassy area between the outer stone walls of the castle

23
00:01:36,147 --> 00:01:37,450
and the river.

24
00:01:37,450 --> 00:01:41,360
We knew then we were onto something.

25
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:46,080
Over the three summers the team has been here, we’ve found the remains of

26
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:47,245
several buildings,

27
00:01:47,245 --> 00:01:50,660
and more broken pots than you can count!

28
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:55,117
Normally you’d expect to find brooches and other jewellery,

29
00:01:55,117 --> 00:01:58,710
but we’re still waiting to uncover any such items.

30
00:01:58,710 --> 00:02:03,632
The people who once lived here were skilled at making tools from animal bones,

31
00:02:03,632 --> 00:02:07,650
as you’ll see when you visit the exhibition.

32
00:02:07,650 --> 00:02:13,466
Besides the discovery of the village, we’ve also found evidence of human activity on

33
00:02:13,466 --> 00:02:17,918
the other side of the river – no other houses or huts so far,

34
00:02:17,918 --> 00:02:22,470
but we can see the borders of an ancient field system.

35
00:02:22,470 --> 00:02:26,249
At one point we found a long wall and thought it was an ancient

36
00:02:26,249 --> 00:02:26,771
palace,

37
00:02:26,771 --> 00:02:30,570
but it turned out to be a modern wall.

38
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:33,413
This summer’s work will end soon,

39
00:02:33,413 --> 00:02:35,450
but we’ll be back next summer.

40
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:41,183
In the meantime, we’re putting on a series of guided tours for school groups

41
00:02:41,183 --> 00:02:42,410
this autumn.

42
00:02:42,410 --> 00:02:47,340
Oh and, maybe you saw the TV documentary about our project.

43
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:52,628
That suggested the objects we’ve found are going to the town’s museum,

44
00:02:52,628 --> 00:02:54,940
but we don’t know that for sure yet.

45
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:59,202
When you enter the site, please make sure you keep to the paths at

46
00:02:59,202 --> 00:03:00,830
all times.

47
00:03:00,830 --> 00:03:04,856
There are a few other things – the highlights of the site, if you

48
00:03:04,856 --> 00:03:07,130
like – that I want to mention.

49
00:03:07,130 --> 00:03:08,336
Take a look at the map;

50
00:03:08,336 --> 00:03:13,320
our present location is marked at the bottom.

51
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:18,310
This year we’ve identified the foundations of an ancient bridge.

52
00:03:18,310 --> 00:03:23,899
And it’s really exciting today because a team of divers are in the river

53
00:03:23,899 --> 00:03:25,970
searching for lost objects.

54
00:03:25,970 --> 00:03:31,086
To reach the bridge, take the main path ahead of you, go straight on

55
00:03:31,086 --> 00:03:35,640
and keep going till the path bends to the left.

56
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:39,580
You’ll see a smaller track leading off to the right.

57
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:44,560
Follow that to take you to the river, where the divers are.

58
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:47,690
You might be interested to see the rubbish pit.

59
00:03:47,690 --> 00:03:53,460
This is very near the castle walls in the northwest corner of the site.

60
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:58,700
It actually dates to the time of the castle and not the ancient village.

61
00:03:58,700 --> 00:04:01,410
We found oyster shells and fish bones,

62
00:04:01,410 --> 00:04:07,210
and we assume they were thrown from the castle kitchen above.

63
00:04:07,210 --> 00:04:12,174
One area we excavated in the first summer uncovered the site of a meeting

64
00:04:12,174 --> 00:04:13,320
hall.

65
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:17,922
We knew it was an important building because it had two rows of post

66
00:04:17,922 --> 00:04:21,700
holes, deep enough to support a large roof.

67
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:27,068
It’s the largest structure in the central area of the site, next to the

68
00:04:27,068 --> 00:04:29,320
current excavation area.

69
00:04:29,320 --> 00:04:33,560
Last year we discovered a fish pond in the ancient village.

70
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:36,350
Normally these were beside a river.

71
00:04:36,350 --> 00:04:38,886
The pond here is further away,

72
00:04:38,886 --> 00:04:42,730
but it’s possible the river has moved slightly.

73
00:04:42,730 --> 00:04:48,633
Anyway, to get there from here, you turn right at the first information board

74
00:04:48,633 --> 00:04:49,355
you come to,

75
00:04:49,355 --> 00:04:53,150
and follow the path into the trees.

76
00:04:53,150 --> 00:04:57,580
Before you come out of the trees, you’ll see it on your right.

77
00:04:57,580 --> 00:05:01,730
If you reach the river, you’ve gone too far.

78
00:05:01,730 --> 00:05:04,280
So, does anyone …
